Subject: Run opcache's JIT under Fil-C by compiling its IR as C
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

Fil-C cannot run machine code written into memory, so opcache's DynASM
backend cannot work. This adds a second backend (ext/opcache/jit/zend_jit_c.c)
used when building with Fil-C: each IR function the JIT produces is printed as
C, compiled by the Fil-C compiler into a shared object and loaded with dlopen.
The compiler instruments the generated code like any other C, so a JIT bug
gives wrong results or a Fil-C panic, never memory corruption.

Fil-C pointers carry an invisible capability that integers lack, and the JIT
treats addresses as integers:

- IR address values are char * in the generated C; additions keep the
  capability of the pointer operand, chosen at run time when both may be one.
- Every pointer the JIT embeds as a constant is registered when it becomes an
  integer (ir_CONST_ADDR, ZEND_ADDR_CONST_ZVAL); generated code reads it back
  from a table the loader fills, and Z_ZV looks it up again.
- The VM's fixed registers (execute_data, opline) become C locals; code that
  the IR reaches by jumping receives them as arguments.

Function JIT works as designed. In the tracing JIT a failed guard copies its
snapshot's values into an emulated register buffer and calls a C version of
the CALL VM's exit stub around zend_jit_trace_exit(). Generated code cannot be
patched, so side traces are compiled but never linked, and exits fall back to
the interpreter; traces also compile synchronously, which makes tracing slower
than the interpreter on short scripts.

The JIT buffer stays ordinary memory (no mprotect), and opcache no longer
picks a mapping hint near PHP's text.

Environment: PHP_JIT_CC (compiler; the default ZEND_JIT_C_CC is meant to be
replaced by an absolute path), PHP_JIT_CFLAGS (-O1), PHP_JIT_C_DIR (/tmp),
PHP_JIT_C_KEEP, PHP_JIT_C_VERBOSE.

